[% PageVersion = 'Documentation/changelog/2026-08-10-infra-ha-load-balancing-session-state.tt,v 1.00 2026/08/10 shanta Exp' %]
[% IF c.session.debug_mode == 1 %][% PageVersion %][% END %]

[% META title = 'INFRA-HA &mdash; Load balancing reality, and two state blockers found in the code' %]
[% META description = 'Records what Kubernetes load balancing does and does not do per request, and documents two code findings that block HA replicas: the session store is Catalyst Session::Store::Dummy (a test store) and several controllers use pod-local /tmp for cross-request state' %]
[% META author = 'shanta' %]
[% META date = '2026-08-10' %]
[% META roles = 'admin devops developer' %]

<div class="changelog-entry">
    <header class="changelog-header">
        <h1>INFRA-HA &mdash; Load balancing reality, and two state blockers</h1>
        <div class="changelog-meta">
            <span class="date">August 10, 2026</span>
            <span class="author">by shanta</span>
            <span class="type">Planning / INFRA-HA (#202) + AUTH-EVO (#243)</span>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="changelog-summary">
        <h2>Summary</h2>
        <p>
            Two questions were put to the
            <a href="[% c.uri_for('/Documentation/K3S_APP_HA_PLAN') %]">Availability &amp;
            Load Balancing Master Plan</a>: does Kubernetes actually pick the best container
            per request, and if consecutive calls land on different containers, does session
            and temp-file storage have to change? Answering the second question required
            reading the code, which surfaced <strong>two blockers the plan did not know
            about</strong>. Documentation and todos only &mdash; <strong>no code or
            infrastructure was changed</strong>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="changelog-details">
        <h2>&sect;3h &mdash; what k8s load balancing does, stated precisely</h2>
        <p>
            An earlier verbal answer in this workstream overstated the limitation by saying
            Kubernetes &ldquo;does not load balance&rdquo;. Corrected and recorded: it does.
            kube-proxy spreads connections across all Ready pods, readiness probes pull broken
            pods from rotation, resource requests and anti-affinity make placement
            capacity-aware, and HPA scales out under load.
        </p>
        <p>
            The single thing not enabled by default is <em>per-request least-connection
            selection</em> &mdash; the default is round-robin, which does not measure backend
            CPU or latency. That is one ingress configuration line (NGINX
            <code>least_conn</code>), not a missing capability.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Deliberately deferred.</strong> This is an availability plan. The
            2026-08-07 outage was a container that did not exist for 100 minutes, not a
            container that was slow. Readiness probes plus <code>replicas=2</code> close that
            outage class entirely; least-request routing addresses a throughput problem the
            estate does not have. &sect;3h records the revisit trigger: enable it if E.4
            (testing under load, AI workload saturating a node) shows uneven latency.
        </p>
        <p>
            &sect;3h also clarifies that the host load figures in &sect;3d are
            <em>placement</em> evidence for a one-time human decision, not runtime inputs.
            Nothing re-reads them per request, and a 1-minute-decayed load average would be
            the wrong signal for routing anyway.
        </p>

        <h2>BLOCKER 1 &mdash; the session store is a test store (&sect;6a)</h2>
        <p>
            <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv.pm</code> line 22 loads
            <code>Session::Store::Dummy</code>, whose own documentation reads
            <em>&ldquo;Doesn't really store sessions - useful for tests.&rdquo;</em> It holds
            session data in a per-process hash in memory.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is worse than &sect;6's original &ldquo;node-local sessions&rdquo; wording:
            sessions are <strong>process-local</strong>. Two replicas would not share a login
            for one user, so every round-robin hop is a potential logout. It also means
            <strong>sessions do not survive a container restart today</strong>, on the current
            single container &mdash; testable now, independent of any HA work.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The fix is already staged and was never switched on.</strong> The
            <code>Plugin::Session</code> config directly beneath the plugin list fully
            specifies a database store (<code>dbic_class =&gt; 'DBEncy::Session'</code>,
            <code>expires_column</code>, <code>id_column</code>, <code>data_column</code>),
            <code>Result/Session.pm</code> exists mapping the <code>sessions</code> table, and
            <code>Catalyst::Plugin::Session::Store::DBIC</code> v0.14 is installed on the host.
            A comment at line 218 claims the store is &ldquo;properly configured&rdquo;; it is
            not, and that comment should go with the fix.
        </p>
        <p>
            A second defect sits in the same area: the cookie name derives from the port
            (<code>comserv_session_$port</code>), so the 5000/3000/4000 instances on .199 issue
            differently named cookies and cannot see each other's sessions even with a shared
            store. The two call sites also disagree on the fallback default (3001 vs 4001).
            Balanced replicas must pin <code>COMSERV_SESSION_COOKIE</code> explicitly.
        </p>
        <p>
            Per &sect;11 this plan states the requirement and does not implement it. Raised as
            AUTH-R2 evidence on
            <a href="[% c.uri_for('/project/details') %]?project_id=243">#243 (AUTH-EVO)</a>,
            todo <a href="[% c.uri_for('/todo/details') %]?record_id=2046">#2046</a>.
            Switching a session store logs out every active user, so it needs a scheduled
            window.
        </p>

        <h2>BLOCKER 2 &mdash; pod-local /tmp holding cross-request state (&sect;6b)</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Uploads are already safe.</strong> They go through
            <code>$c-&gt;path_to('root','uploads',...)</code> and the NFS mount
            (<code>NFS_HOST_PATH</code> &rarr; <code>/data/nfs</code>, present in both compose
            services). Mount the same NFS into both pods and a file uploaded via pod A is
            visible from pod B.
        </p>
        <p>
            The exposure is <code>/tmp</code> files that a <em>later</em> request reads back.
            A grep of the controllers found four:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>HIGH</strong> &mdash; <code>/tmp/whisper_job_${job_id}.status</code> and
                <code>_result.json</code> (AI.pm ~12293&ndash;12332): submit-then-poll, where the
                poll lands on the other pod and the job appears lost.</li>
            <li><strong>HIGH</strong> &mdash; <code>/tmp/comserv_chat_progress_${session_id}</code>
                (AI.pm ~8200, 8233): same failure for chat progress.</li>
            <li><strong>MEDIUM</strong> &mdash; <code>/tmp/comserv_security_scan.{txt,json}</code>
                (Admin.pm ~2828&ndash;3042): scan writes on one pod, results page reads the other.</li>
            <li><strong>MEDIUM</strong> &mdash; <code>/tmp/comserv_deploy_4000.pid</code>
                (Planning.pm ~1702): a PID file is meaningless across pods.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>File::Temp</code> with <code>UNLINK =&gt; 1</code> (AI.pm ~646) is
            <strong>fine as-is</strong> &mdash; written and consumed inside one request. That is
            the dividing rule now recorded in the plan: scratch within a request may stay in
            pod-local <code>/tmp</code>; anything read back later is shared state.
        </p>
        <p>
            Tracked as todo
            <a href="[% c.uri_for('/todo/details') %]?record_id=2047">#2047</a>, blocking
            Phase C. This is independent of the session fix &mdash; sharing sessions does not
            help a poll looking for a file on the wrong pod's disk.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="changelog-details">
        <h2>Why this matters beyond HA</h2>
        <p>
            Both findings are live defects, not just future obstacles. Sessions already fail to
            survive a container restart, and the <code>/tmp</code> job-status pattern already
            breaks if the background worker and the polling endpoint are ever separated. HA
            work surfaced them; it did not cause them.
        </p>
    </section>

